WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.350
When an alert turns out to be a real attack, the instinct is to fix it instantly — kill the process, delete the file, wipe the machine.

2
00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:13.170
Skilled analysts resist that instinct. The first job is containment.

3
00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:23.410
Containment means stopping the incident from getting worse — cutting off the attacker's access, movement, or communication — while preserving the evidence you'll need later.

4
00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:31.520
Eradication — removing the malware — and recovery — restoring service — come after, once you understand the scope.

5
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.550
Think of a hospital discovering a contagious patient.

6
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:44.690
It doesn't start surgery. It first moves the patient to an isolation ward so the infection can't spread — and only then diagnoses and treats.

7
00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:53.160
Treat before you isolate, and the infection spreads while you're distracted. Same order in a SOC: contain before you clean up.

8
00:00:53.660 --> 00:01:05.640
There are three containment actions a Tier 1 or Tier 2 analyst is most often trusted to run directly. First, isolate the host — cut it off the network, but keep it powered on so the evidence survives.

9
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:12.550
Second, disable the compromised account and revoke its active sessions, so stolen credentials stop working.

10
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:18.550
Third, block the indicators — the malicious IP, domain, or hash — at the network edge.

11
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:24.400
Every one of those actions is built around an IOC — an Indicator of Compromise.

12
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:34.240
An IOC is any observable artifact that suggests malicious activity — an IP address, a file hash, a domain, a registry key, a command line.

13
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:42.280
In NIST's incident-response guidance, these actions all live in the Respond function — contain, then eradicate, then recover.

14
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.630
So before you clean anything up: stop the bleeding, and keep the evidence.

15
00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:53.850
Isolate the host, disable the account, block the IOC — then investigate and eradicate.
